{
  "summary": "The converged design has six unresolved implementation hazards: (1) manual override detection via history scanning is O(h) per feature per broadcast cycle and fragile against history format changes — a dedicated `override: bool` field on FeatureFile set by _cmd_move_feature when moving blocked features into impl_stages would be cheaper and more reliable; (2) the _dependency_dirty flag optimization is largely ineffective because stage transitions (which set the flag dirty) happen on nearly every auto-run cycle, so the blocked-status cache is rebuilt almost every time anyway — the flag should track depends_on edits separately from stage transitions, and stage-transition dirtying should be scoped to only terminal-stage transitions (done/rejected) that actually change resolution status; (3) the plan_invalidation_check has a race with _auto_plan_queued — if auto-run picks up feature B for planning (adding it to _auto_plan_queued) and then dependency A is rejected in the same cycle, plan_invalidation_check moves B to plan-inbox but the already-queued planning task proceeds with stale context, producing a plan that assumes A exists; the invalidation check must also cancel or poison queued planning tasks, not just move the feature; (4) dependency_events as an append-only list with per-event 'addressed' marking will grow unboundedly over a feature's lifetime with no compaction strategy — a feature that goes through multiple rejection/re-planning cycles accumulates dead entries indefinitely, and the planning prompt must filter through all of them each time; (5) sorting candidates by len(unresolved_deps) is a count-based heuristic, not a topological sort — it does not guarantee that if A depends on B and both are in 'approved' with all other deps resolved, B is processed before A (both would have 0 or equal unresolved_deps if B's own dependencies are met), so the 'dependency-free features processed first' guarantee from round 10 does not actually hold; a proper topological ordering using Kahn's algorithm or DFS post-order on the dependency subgraph of current candidates is needed; (6) the resolve_dependencies() return schema specifies {blocked, unresolved_deps} per feature but the proposal also requires dangling dependencies to be surfaced as a distinct error state — the return value needs a separate dangling_deps list alongside unresolved_deps so callers (auto-run skip logging, broadcast state, TUI display) can distinguish between 'dependency exists but not yet resolved' and 'dependency ID points to nothing' without re-running the resolution logic."
}
